Vertical column on a self-built block-and-extrusion table frame at 550 mm,
140 mm fan under the bottom deck blowing upward. 15x 50 mm pine cubes ordered
to evaluate before committing.

- Cube nodes: square by manufacture (removes the hand-cut error that put the
  built box 2 mm under), and lossy wood breaks the extrusion's ringing path.
- 50 mm legs leave only 30 mm under the 20 mm fan (0.21D) - noisier than
  needed. Double the legs (needs 16 cubes) or cut four to length.
- Extrusion continuous through nodes: bonds in shear, not peel; tolerance
  stops accumulating.
- Deck strip-built, 8 pieces, no notching. Core seams at 205/345 mm coincide
  with where cross members want to be. Second deck rotated 90 degrees.
  Aperture 145 not 140, foam collar = decoupling for free.
- USB buck-boost closes the long-open 5 V start problem and is fail-safe by
  being dumb. Must not be powered from a machine in the rack.
- Annex 120s add no capacity unless each annex gets its own intake.
  Three chambers across 400 mm caps annex fans at 120 mm.
- 19 inch compatibility explicitly ruled out.

Open: extrusion type (T-slot would dissolve platerack.py's socket scheme);
whether annexes vent outside or back into the central volume.
