+## FRAME + DECK SCHEME — block-and-extrusion table frame (2026-08-21)
+
+**The design has moved to a vertical column on a self-built table frame: wood
+cube nodes bonded to extrusion, with the 140 mm fan under the bottom deck
+blowing upward.** This supersedes the cast-monocoque assumptions about how the
+walls carry load — the *frame* is now the structure and the walls hang on it.
+
+**⚠ NOT YET COMMITTED TO — 15× 50 mm pine cubes are ORDERED (2026-08-21) so
+Peter can look at one before proceeding.** Everything below is the scheme the
+cubes are being evaluated against, not a locked design. 15 covers 12 nodes plus
+spares; it does **not** cover doubled legs (see below).
+
+### Why cubes at the nodes, and why wood
+
+- **Bought cubes are square by manufacture**, which removes the accuracy
+  problem. The built box already came in 2 mm under design from hand-cutting,
+  and a frame accumulates that error across twelve members. This was the whole
+  reason for choosing cubes over cut blocks.
+- **Wood nodes are acoustically load-bearing.** Extrusion is stiff and *rings*
+  — the same failure mode that ruled out 6 mm ply for the walls. Lossy wood
+  blocks at every node break the ringing path between members. Constrained-layer
+  damping, arrived at for free.
+- **⚠ Check the arrises on arrival.** Craft cubes are often tumbled/sanded, and
+  a rounded edge means the extrusion doesn't seat flat — glue area is lost
+  exactly at the perimeter where the moment is carried. Want sawn or machined.
+  Check squareness with a small square; 1° at a node is ~1 mm over 50 mm.
+
+### Geometry — 550 mm, matching LACK, but NOT for 19"
+
+**Peter has ruled out 19" rack compatibility** — so the outer dimension is free,
+and 550 mm (LACK footprint) is the choice. For the record, since it will come up
+again: a LACK side table is 550 × 550 × 450 h, legs ~35 mm square, inner span
+~480 mm ≈ 19" (the LackRack coincidence), top ~50 mm thick, clear height under
+the top ~400 mm. Legs are hollow except the top ~50 mm. **Not the route taken.**
+
+| | With 50 mm cubes |
+|---|---|
+| Outer | 550 × 550 |
+| Inner span (cross members between blocks) | **450 mm** |
+| Cross members continuous, full width | 550 mm |
+| Clearance around the 414 mm box | 18 mm a side |
+
+**Extrusion should run CONTINUOUS through the nodes, not in segments between
+them.** Then the extrusion sets all geometry and cube length tolerance is
+cosmetic; bonds are in **shear** (strong) against flat faces rather than in
+**peel** against cut ends (weak). Segmented extrusion stacks twelve length
+errors into the overall height.
+
+**⚠ Racking is the frame's weak axis.** Four posts + cross members with no
+diagonals is a mechanism. Bonded 50 mm cube faces are a real moment connection,
+but at least two adjacent faces still want a **shear panel** (a deck fixed to
+all four posts, or the walls bonded rather than hung).
+
+**Glue: epoxy, not PU** (PU foams and is weak in the gap). If the extrusion is
+aluminium, the oxide re-forms in minutes — abrade, degrease, bond immediately.
+
+### ⚠ Leg height: 50 mm cubes are SHORT for the fan
+
+The fan is **20 mm thick, mounted under the bottom deck**. A single 50 mm cube
+leg leaves **30 mm** under the fan — that is 0.21D on a 140 mm bore, under the
+0.25D rule of thumb for undisturbed inflow. Free area is still ample
+(~500 cm² of perimeter gap vs ~154 cm² of fan), so it will not *throttle* — but
+it will be **noisier than it needs to be, and noise is the entire point of the
+box**.
+
+Fixes, in order of preference: **stack two cubes at each leg** (100 mm, leaving
+80 mm clear — and it doubles as a live test of the glue joint), or cut only the
+four legs to length and use cubes for the eight upper nodes. **Doubling the legs
+needs 16 cubes, not 15** — order is one short if that route is taken.
+
+### Deck — strip-built, 8 pieces, no notching
+
+Rather than a 550 × 550 sheet with four 50 mm corner notches (four internal
+corners, each needing overcut to seat on a sharp-cornered block), the deck is
+**built up from strips around a central fan aperture**. Per deck:
+
+| Qty | Size (mm) | Role |
+|---|---|---|
+| 2 | 450 × 155 | core, either side of aperture |
+| 2 | 155 × 140 | infill, above and below aperture |
+| 4 | 450 × 50 | edge strips, between the legs |
+
+Two decks come off one 1220 × 610 handy panel. 12 mm ply; 9 mm drums under
+disk vibration.
+
+**The seams land where the cross members want to be anyway.** Core seams sit at
+**205 mm and 345 mm** from the frame edge — exactly the two sides of the fan
+aperture. A pair of intermediate cross members there backs the butt joints *and*
+frames the fan. Design to this rather than discovering it later.
+
+**Rotate the second deck 90°.** Butt joints are weak alone, but with the second
+layer quarter-turned no seam sits over a seam. Plywood logic; the largest free
+strength gain available here.
+
+**⚠ Cut the aperture at 145, not 140.** A 140 mm fan frame is 140.0 nominal, so
+a 140 hole is an interference fit that wood movement turns into a jam or a
+rattle. 145 + a closed-cell foam collar gives **decoupling for free** — no
+metal-to-wood path into the deck every machine stands on — and makes the
+aperture tolerance stop mattering, which suits a strip-built panel. Free area
+~210 cm² vs the fan's ~127 cm² swept bore, so the deck is not the restriction.
+
+**Any solid deck in a bottom-intake vertical-flow box is a plug.** The notches
+don't help — they are filled by the legs. Whichever deck scheme wins, apertures
+must be sized against the fan (≥ fan area, ideally 2×) so the deck acts as a
+**diffuser**, not a barrier. Card baffle for flow division, kept well clear of
+the PSU chamber (card is worse than the foam already excluded there).
+
+### Fan power — USB + adjustable buck-boost (2026-08-21)
+
+**Peter is running the 140 mm fan from USB via an adjustable buck-boost
+converter. This CLOSES the long-open "12 V fans may not start at 5 V" item** —
+set 7–8 V and get full starting torque while still running slow and quiet.
+Tune to the lowest voltage that starts reliably **from cold after sitting
+overnight**, not from warm; cold start is the failure case.
+
+It is also **fail-safe by being dumb**: no Pi, no duty cycle, nothing to crash.
+Power on means fan spinning. This satisfies the fail-safe rule better than PWM
+control does — keep it this way.
+
+- **⚠ Inrush, not running current, is the risk.** ~0.6 A at the 5 V input is
+  nothing, but the converter's output caps look like a near-short at power-on
+  and can trip host port current limiting — the port latches off and the fan is
+  dead with no other symptom. Soft-start, or bring the converter up before
+  connecting the fan; never hot-plug.
+- **⚠ Do NOT power it from a USB port on a machine in the rack.** The fan would
+  die whenever that machine sleeps, crashes or is pulled for a swap — a silent
+  thermal failure on the box holding the data, exactly the class of fault that
+  got puppy. Use a dedicated USB PSU on the Zigbee plug: that plug is already
+  set start-on-power On, so `rack-power cycle` resets the fan with everything
+  else.
+- **Open: no tach feedback.** `thermal/pico_fan_driver/` already exists —
+  repurposing it to *read* tach rather than drive PWM is a smaller job and
+  closes the silent-failure hole without putting anything in the power path.
+
+### Annexes — separate chambers, own decks, internal 120s
+
+Plan: a central chamber with the 140 under it, **annexes to the side with their
+own decks and their own 120 mm fans**, drawing air across.
+
+**⚠ A fan inside the box adds NO cooling capacity — it only redistributes what
+the 140 brings in.** If the annexes draw from the central volume and dump back
+into it, the 120s are pure recirculation (~0 W/K net export) and total export
+stays ~8 W/K, i.e. ~19 °C rise at 150 W — marginal, since a disk at 40 °C
+gaining 19 °C is at its limit. They are still worth having: the disks have no
+heatsink and need air *moving across* them regardless of where it goes.
+
+If instead an annex exhausts through a side wall, its 120 is in **series** with
+the 140 on that branch — pressure adds, flow does not — and a 120 pulling harder
+than the 140 supplies will draw air backwards through its own exhaust and
+breathe its own warm air.
+
+**Resolution: give each annex its own intake.** Then the 120s are genuine
+parallel paths, flow adds, each chamber's budget is independent, and a failed
+annex fan is a local problem. This is the same answer the strand already reached
+for the disks ("give them their own intake, don't reorder the stack").
+
+**⚠ Chamber width now sets fan size — harder than the hole-saw constraint.**
+Three chambers across 400 mm internal with two partitions gives ~129 mm each:
+**a 140 mm fan does not fit; 120 mm does.** So the three-chamber decision
+quietly settles the long-open fan-size question at **120 mm** — unless chambers
+are sized unequally to their contents (the plate rack is the width-hungry one;
+a disk annex could be narrow).
+
+**Open — does each annex vent to outside, or back into the central volume?**
+This single answer determines whether the 120s are capacity or circulation, and
+whether more intake area than one 140 is needed. Side exhausts also mean four
+more apertures in the least reversible material, which interacts with the
+structural limit on how much can be removed from a cast wall.
+
+### Sourcing
+
+- **Timber:** Alsford, St Mark's Hill, Surbiton (020 8399 4234) — closest proper
+  merchant, will cut and keeps offcuts. Closes noon Sat, shut Sun. Backups:
+  Lords and Selco, both Richmond Rd, Kingston (Selco open Sun 10–4).
+- **Nominal is not actual:** UK "2×2" planed finishes at **44 × 44**, sawn
+  ~47 × 47. True 50 × 50 must be asked for explicitly. Moot while cubes are the
+  route, but it is the trap if cut blocks are ever revisited.
+- **UK T-slot cut-to-length** (if aluminium extrusion is chosen over steel
+  square tube): KJN, Motedis, Ooznest, all to ~±0.5 mm. **T-slot would dissolve
+  the plate rack's fixed-socket problem** — fins would slide at continuous
+  pitch, making the 10 mm sockets, two comb heights and `top_comb_z()` grip
+  check unnecessary. Steel square tube keeps the comb as designed. **Undecided,
+  and it gates whether `platerack.py` gets simplified.**
+
+### Next actions for this scheme
+
+1. **Look at a cube when the 15 arrive** — squareness, arris, finish. Gate on
+   this before anything else.
+2. **Decide doubled legs vs cut legs** (and order a 16th cube if doubling).
+3. **Choose the extrusion** — steel square tube or 2020 T-slot. Gates
+   `platerack.py`.
+4. Confirm continuous-through-node routing, then work the frame BOM and cut
+   list.
+
